\subsubsection{Adaptation mechanism}
Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_channels} retains all four channel-ablation arms in the
main text. At $H_s=\SI{8.5}{m}$, full adaptation, $r_S$ only, OU only, and
FixedNominal yield $3.95$, $4.09$, $11.52$, and $13.26\%$ of $H_s$,
respectively. Adaptive $r_S$ captures most of the off-nominal benefit;
OU-only adaptation helps less, rather than not at all. At the nominal sea,
Adaptive minus FixedNominal is
$\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalDifference$ percentage points
($[\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalLow,\OUValidationIIIHsOneFiveNominalHigh]$).

Disabling periodic latent-acceleration covariance re-alignment produces a
largest-magnitude paired change of
$\OUValidationCovSyncWorstDifference$ percentage points
($[\OUValidationCovSyncWorstLow,\OUValidationCovSyncWorstHigh]$).
That maintenance policy does not explain the substantially larger
adaptation-channel effects.

\subsubsection{Deterministic baselines}
Adaptive PII, OU--II, the two-frame Lie-group filter (TFG), and the adapted
time-varying-gain nonlinear observer (TVG--NLO)
\cite{BryneFossenJohansen2014_TVG_NLO} replay identical timestamped synthetic motion and inertial records.
The TVG--NLO configuration omits the published observer's horizontal GNSS
and compass aiding; its horizontal position and yaw are unscored.
The common endpoints are vertical displacement and tilt
(Table~\ref{tab:baseline-tuning-policy}). Comparator coefficients are
selected offline on the vessel-response records, then held as frozen parameters for evaluation on additional
sensor/initialization draws; reference displacement never enters online
scheduling or gains. OU--III is fixed during comparator selection. These are development
ensemble comparisons, not unseen-vessel tests.
OU--II and OU--III share the wave-band period input but use PhysicalMSE dual
and SpectralMSE integral regularization, respectively.

